---
# Example Ansible Vault file for connection credentials.
#
# These variables are referenced from hosts.ini (see the commented
# "vault-based access" example). Ansible does NOT auto-load this file:
# under group_vars/all/ it auto-loads files that have no extension or a
# .yml/.yaml/.json extension, so the ".example" suffix keeps this file out of
# the loaded variables and it is safe to keep as a plaintext template in the
# repository.
#
# To enable vault-based credentials:
#
#   1. Copy this file to group_vars/all/vault.yml with owner-only access, so
#      the real credentials you add in step 2 are never readable by other
#      local users while the file is still plaintext (a plain "cp" would
#      create it world-readable under the default umask):
#        install -m 600 group_vars/all/vault.yml.example group_vars/all/vault.yml
#   2. Replace the placeholder values below with your real credentials.
#   3. Encrypt the file with your own vault password:
#        ansible-vault encrypt group_vars/all/vault.yml
#   4. Activate the vault-based inventory entry in hosts.ini: uncomment the
#      "vault-based access" node line (setting ansible_user), and comment out
#      or remove the plain default line for that node. Without this step the
#      node still has no ansible_password/ansible_sudo_pass and vault-based
#      login is not used.
#   5. Tell Ansible where to find the matching vault password. OnRamp drives
#      Ansible through Make targets (e.g. "make aether-pingall") whose recipes
#      do not forward --ask-vault-pass, so Make-based use requires a vault
#      password file: create one and uncomment "vault_password_file" in
#      ansible.cfg. (--ask-vault-pass works only for direct "ansible-playbook"
#      invocations.)
#      The file must contain, in plaintext, the same password you passed to
#      "ansible-vault encrypt" above; an empty file cannot decrypt vault.yml.
#      Because it holds that plaintext password, create it with owner-only
#      access first (the default umask may otherwise leave it world-readable),
#      then write the password without recording it in your shell history:
#        install -m 600 /dev/null ~/.ansible_vault_pass
#        read -rsp 'Vault password: ' vault_pw && \
#          printf '%s\n' "$vault_pw" > ~/.ansible_vault_pass && unset vault_pw
#
# group_vars/all/vault.yml is git-ignored so your encrypted secrets are
# never committed.

vault_password: "<ssh-password>"
vault_sudo_password: "<sudo-password>"
